///1VI
VI - STevie editor for RETRO86 CP/M-86 and VT100/ANSI

This is the supplied cpm86-vi editor adapted for the RETRO86 emulator.
It is a native CP/M-86 command, not a DOS executable or a CP/M-80 .COM.
The original vi editing engine is retained. Screen and keyboard access
use CP/M BDOS and VT100/ANSI sequences, without PC BIOS calls.

Start: VI NOTES.TXT
Help:  HELP VI
Inside VI: H or :help followed by Return.
Exit safely: Escape, :wq, Return.
Exit without saving: Escape, :q!, Return.

This is a small STevie implementation, not modern Vim. Its command set
is described below. Commands are case-sensitive.

///2QUICKSTART
FIRST EDITING SESSION

  A>VI NOTES.TXT

VI starts in Normal mode. Typing letters there executes commands.
Press i to enter Insert mode. Now type your text; Return starts a line.
Press Escape to return to Normal mode.

Move with h j k l, or use the arrow keys. To save and exit, type:

  :wq

Press Return after a colon command. The colon opens the command line;
it is not part of the filename. Use :w to save and continue. Use :q to
quit after saving. If text is still changed, :q refuses to discard it.
Use :q! only when you intend to discard the current changes.

Example:
  VI TEST.TXT
  i
  Hello from CP/M-86!
  [Escape]
  :wq [Return]

[Escape] and [Return] mean keys, not literal text to type.

///2MODES
NORMAL, INSERT AND REPLACE MODES

Normal mode: keys move the cursor or operate on text. Examples are
w (next word), x (delete character), dd (delete line), i (insert).

Insert mode: i, a, I, A, o or O starts text entry. Return inserts a
newline. Tab inserts four spaces. Ctrl-V followed by Tab inserts an
actual tab character. Backspace removes text entered in the current
insertion group; it does not freely erase earlier existing text.
Escape ends the insertion and returns to Normal mode.

Replace mode: R starts overwriting existing characters. At a line end,
new characters can be inserted. Backspace restores overwritten text
from the current replace group. Escape returns to Normal mode.

In this RETRO86 build, a navigation key used in Insert or Replace mode
first ends that editing group, then performs its Normal-mode action.
For example, an arrow moves the cursor and leaves VI in Normal mode;
press i or a to resume typing. This preserves contiguous undo groups
and prevents terminal escape sequences from entering the file.

The bottom row shows mode, status, warnings or a command prompt.
Escape also cancels a colon/search prompt. A lone Escape in Normal
mode does nothing. Its recognition uses a bounded polling delay.

///2TERMINAL
TERMINAL AND EMULATOR SETTINGS

Use an 80-column, 24-row VT100/ANSI terminal. Larger terminal windows
work, but VI uses only the first 80 columns and 24 rows. The first
23 rows display text; the last row is the status/command line.
Long lines wrap across screen rows. Tildes mark unused screen lines.

VI uses absolute cursor positioning, ANSI screen clearing and reverse
video. Automatic wrapping is disabled while VI runs, so writing the
last screen column does not cause an unwanted terminal scroll.
On normal exit, attributes and scroll region are reset, wrapping is
enabled and the cursor is shown. Ctrl-L redraws the editor display.

The terminal must forward keystrokes without local echo. Disable
software flow control so Control keys reach the guest. On a Mac,
stty -ixon can disable host XON/XOFF for the current terminal session.
Avoid large fast pastes over a serial link: host/emulator queues can
be smaller than the pasted text. Bracketed-paste mode is disabled.
Pasted text is processed as ordinary VI keystrokes, not as a separate
paste operation; enter Insert mode first and respect insertion limits.

Supported input includes ESC[ and ESCO arrows, H/F Home/End,
1~/7~ Home, 4~/8~ End, 2~ Insert, 3~ Delete, 5~ Page Up and 6~ Page Down.
Common modifier parameters on arrow sequences are accepted as the
base key. Unknown complete escape sequences are ignored.
Legacy ESC[I is also accepted as Page Up.

No IBM video BIOS, keyboard BIOS, PC screen memory or terminal-specific
DRI keyboard expansion is required. This build always uses BDOS input.
The console remains byte-oriented; this is not a Unicode editor.

///2MOVEMENT
MOVEMENT IN NORMAL MODE

h / Left       Move left one character.
j / Down       Move down one logical line.
k / Up         Move up one logical line.
l / Right      Move right one character.
b              Back one word.
w              Forward one word.
0 or ^ / Home  Beginning of line. Here ^ is the caret character.
$ / End        End of line.
G              Go to the last line.
25G            Go to line 25; use another number as required.
:25            Also go to line 25; finish with Return.
Ctrl-F         Forward one screen (23 logical lines).
Ctrl-B         Back one screen (23 logical lines).
Ctrl-D         Down half a screen (11 logical lines).
Ctrl-U         Up half a screen (11 logical lines).
Page Down      Same as Ctrl-F.
Page Up        Same as Ctrl-B.
Ctrl-G         Show filename, changed state and position.
Ctrl-L         Clear and redraw the screen.

Only commands explicitly supporting a count should be assumed to use
one. This small implementation does not implement every Vim motion,
operator combination, mark, register or window command.

///2INSERT
INSERT, APPEND AND REPLACE

i       Insert before the cursor.
I       Insert before the first nonblank character of the line.
a       Append after the cursor.
A       Append at the end of the line.
o       Open a line below and enter Insert mode.
O       Open a line above and enter Insert mode.
rX      Replace the character under the cursor with X.
R       Enter Replace mode; Escape ends it.
Insert  Same as i in Normal mode.

In Insert mode, Return inserts LF internally, and Tab inserts four
spaces. Ctrl-V quotes the next byte, for example Ctrl-V Tab. Text-mode
saving converts internal LF to CRLF. Leaving Insert at logical EOF
can add a final newline, as in the original editor.

Ctrl-X followed by two hexadecimal digits inserts a byte by value.
For example, Ctrl-X 4 1 inserts A. Ctrl-V and hexadecimal byte entry
are advanced features: embedded control bytes may change file meaning.
Do not use them to put terminal navigation sequences into the file.

An insertion group has a fixed history buffer of about 1 KiB. Near
that limit VI refuses further input and shows a warning. Press Escape,
then i or a, to start a new group. The whole file limit is separate.

///2DELETE
DELETE AND CHANGE

x / Delete   Delete the character under the cursor.
DEL byte 127 Same as x in Normal mode; Backspace in text entry.
dd           Delete the current line.
3dd          Delete three lines, starting with the current line.
dw           Delete the current word and trailing spaces.
D or d$      Delete from cursor to the end of the line.
cc           Change the current line: delete its text and enter Insert.
cw           Change the current word, entering Insert mode.
C or c$      Change from cursor to end of line, entering Insert mode.
J            Join the current line with the next line.

For changes, type replacement text and press Escape when finished.
Line deletion uses the linewise buffer, which can be put back with p/P.
Word/EOL deletion is refused when its undo text would exceed about
1 KiB; use whole-line operations for exceptionally long lines.

This is not a complete Vim operator grammar. Do not assume commands
such as d%, visual selection, named registers or ex ranges exist.

///2COPY
YANK, PUT, UNDO AND REPEAT

yy       Yank (copy) the current line.
3yy      Yank three lines.
p        Put the saved lines after the current line.
P        Put the saved lines before the current line.
u        Undo the last supported change.
.        Repeat the last supported insertion/deletion command.
>>       Indent the current line right by one tab.
<<       Remove one leading tab from the current line.
3>>      Indent three lines right.
~        Toggle case of the character under the cursor.
5~       Toggle case of five characters.

Yank/delete operations share a single linewise buffer. A later yank
or line deletion replaces it. There are no named registers or a
multi-level undo history. Undo/repeat support follows this small
STevie implementation; save separate copies for important versions.
The line buffer and edit history are lost on program exit.

///2SEARCH
SEARCHING

/pattern [Return]  Search forward for literal text.
?pattern [Return]  Search backward for literal text.
n                 Repeat the previous search in its previous direction.
// [Return]       Repeat the previous search from a forward prompt.
?? [Return]       Repeat the previous search from a backward prompt.

Searches are case-sensitive, support overlapping matches and wrap once
around the file. A failed search reports Pattern not found.
These are literal string searches, not regular expressions. There is
no :s substitution command. Escape cancels the search prompt.

Backspace edits the prompt; Ctrl-U clears it. The inherited prompt
also treats @ as a line-clear key, so @ cannot be typed there normally.
Prompt input is bounded to keep long commands within its fixed buffer.

///2COMMANDS
COLON COMMAND REFERENCE

Press Escape first to leave text entry, then type the colon command.
Finish each command with Return. Commands and filenames are separate
arguments; spaces are not permitted inside CP/M filenames.

:w             Save under the current filename.
:w FILE.TXT    Write the buffer to another filename.
:wq            Save under the current filename and quit if successful.
:x             Save and quit, like :wq in this implementation.
:q             Quit only if no unsaved changes remain.
:q!            Quit and discard changes unconditionally.
:e FILE.TXT    Edit another file; refuse if the buffer is changed.
:e! FILE.TXT   Discard changes and edit that file.
:e!            Discard changes and reload the current file.
:r FILE.TXT    Insert another file after the current logical line.
:f             Show file information.
:f FILE.TXT    Change the buffer's filename; does not write or rename disk.
:.=            Show current line number and character offset.
:$=            Show the editor's total-line count.
:25            Go to line 25, clamping at the last line if necessary.
:set oct       Show nonprintable characters with octal notation.
:set hex       Show nonprintable characters with hexadecimal notation.
:set dec       Show nonprintable characters with decimal notation.
:v             Show the RETRO86 build identification.
:h or :help    Display built-in help; any key returns to editing.

H also opens built-in help directly from Normal mode.
There is no :! shell command, split window, macro scripting or Vim config.

///2FILES
FILENAMES, TEXT FORMAT AND MEMORY

Use CP/M 8.3 filenames, optionally with a drive prefix:

  VI NOTES.TXT
  VI B:SOURCE.A86

The Aztec CP/M runtime also accepts a user-area prefix:

  VI 3/B:NOTES.TXT

The startup prefix selects that user area for the session. Use valid
CP/M user numbers (normally 0-15). This is CP/M syntax, not a Mac path.
No directories or long filenames are supported. Avoid wildcards.
An unreadable or nonexistent input can be treated as a new file by the
inherited loader; check filename and drive before saving a new buffer.

Text loading stops at Ctrl-Z, discards CR and retains LF internally.
Text saving writes CRLF line endings and a CP/M Ctrl-Z EOF marker,
then pads to whole 128-byte records. An empty text file still contains
an EOF marker. Tabs already in a file are retained; typed Tab inserts
four spaces unless quoted with Ctrl-V.

The file allocation is 24000 bytes. A guard byte is reserved, so the
usable loaded/edited text is at most 23999 bytes after normalization.
Insertion/undo groups have smaller, roughly 1 KiB limits. Very large
files cannot be edited. The program requires code memory plus a full
64 KiB data/stack group, with heap space supplied by the runtime.

Optional -x and -o select hexadecimal or octal display of nonprintable
bytes. -b is inherited binary mode: no CRLF conversion or Ctrl-Z text
EOF handling. On CP/M-86 1.1/2.2, binary lengths are record-rounded;
this build does not make CP/M store an exact binary byte count. Prefer
text mode for source, BASIC, assembly, .SUB and help files.

///2SAVE
SAVE BEHAVIOUR AND RECOVERY

Use :w to save, :wq to save and quit, or :q! to discard changes.
Successful saves clear the changed flag. A failed save keeps that flag,
so :q continues to warn and :wq does not silently exit.

On our CP/M-86 1.1/2.2 system, rewriting an existing file uses a unique
VI000000.TMP-style temporary file. The old original is renamed to a
unique VI000000.BAK-style recovery name before installing the new copy.
After successful installation, the temporary backup is removed.
These names are selected from a bounded pool and may have other digits.

This is a replacement transaction, not a permanent .BAK history. Make
separate backups yourself if older versions must be retained. Creating
a new file writes that new file directly. A write error can leave it
partial; keep the in-memory buffer open while assessing the error.

If a save reports recovery filenames, retain them. After leaving VI,
inspect the original and named .TMP/.BAK files before deleting anything.
A .TMP can contain newly written text, while a .BAK can contain the
previous original. The error can also report the relevant user area.
Free disk space and resolve read-only conditions before retrying.

The runtime retains its upstream save policy for other CP/M variants;
RETRO86 does not need Concurrent CP/M or DOS Plus services.

///2EXAMPLE
SHORT PRACTICE SESSION

Start with a disposable file:
  VI PRACTICE.TXT

Press i, type First line, Return, then Second line. Press Escape.
Type 1G to go to the first line. Type yy to copy it.
Type G to go to the bottom, then p to put a copy after that line.
Type /Second and Return to search for Second.
Type cw, type Another, then Escape to change that word.
Type u to undo the last supported change.
Type :w and Return to save. Type :q and Return to exit.

If anything is confusing, Escape returns to Normal mode. H shows the
built-in reference. :q! exits without saving the current buffer.

///2TROUBLE
TROUBLESHOOTING

Letters execute commands instead of appearing:
  You are in Normal mode. Press i to type text.

An arrow moves but typing stops:
  Navigation ends Insert/Replace in this build. Press i or a to resume.

Escape seems slow or a remote key sequence is incomplete:
  Its bounded polling interval depends on emulator speed. Use h j k l
  for movement and verify the terminal sends complete ANSI sequences.

Screen looks wrong:
  Use an 80x24 VT100/ANSI terminal, check local echo, and press Ctrl-L.
  Do not run the upstream PC BIOS or VT52 binaries in this emulator.

Input stops after a long paste or insertion:
  Check the insertion-group warning. Escape and i start a new group.
  Reduce paste speed and size to fit the serial input queues.

Save fails or :q warns:
  Read the status message. Check disk space, drive, user area and file
  permissions. :q! deliberately discards the unsaved buffer.

File too long:
  The text capacity is 23999 bytes. Loading checks capacity first.
  An oversized startup file is refused; an oversized :r is refused
  without changing the existing buffer. Work on smaller text files.

///2ABOUT
BUILD, SOURCE AND VALIDATION

Based on tsupplis/cpm86-vi, upstream revision:
  201d71cfa6d3ae73e85f45af51f2110a18c4e085

The package includes adapted source, the upstream license and a patch.
Build with the patched Aztec C 4.2 toolchain from cpm86-crossdev:
  CROSSDEV=/path/to/cpm86-crossdev sh BUILD-RETRO.sh
No compiler, runtime installation or Python is needed to run VI.CMD.

RETRO86 changes include the raw BDOS/ANSI console, CSI/SS3 decoder,
terminal restoration, native Control-key motions, bounded command and
insertion input, safe literal repeat-search storage, and save/error
state fixes. Existing vi editing commands remain in the supplied engine.

The actual generated VI.CMD has been exercised in the RETRO86 project's
tiny8086 CPU core with a controlled CP/M BDOS/file-system fixture.
Tests cover creating/saving, undo, searching/repeat, ANSI navigation,
line copying, shorter rewrites and injected write failures. Additional
host tests cover keyboard decoding, scrolling and EOF line operations.
This is not a live ESP32 or full guest-BDOS acceptance test; that final
hardware/terminal check remains to be done.
